> ["]The central issue in this debate is the belief in the existence of a Supreme Being. Julian Huxley,[ grandfather] of Thomas Huxley and[ one] of the foremost evolutionists of his day,[ was an] unabashed atheist. He pointed out in 1959 that Darwin's real achievement was to 'remove the whole idea of God[ as the] Creator of organisms from the spear of rational discussion' (Tax and Callender[ 1960, 3:45]) The belief in[ a] Supreme Being may at once be recognized[ as] the one essential characteristic of[ a the]istic religion[.] It is perhaps surprising , however, to find that the belief in[ a] nonexistence[ of a] Supreme Being is also considered to be a (nontheistic) religion. Thus, atheism was formally declared to[ be a] religion by the United States Supreme Court in 1961, together with Buddhism, Ethical Culture, Secular Humanism, and others that do not teach[ a] belief in the[ existence of] God (Bird[ 1979]). The Atheist Church of America and the American Ethical Union,[ for] example, are both bona fide tax-exempt religious organizations[."]
